records.question:
For
\[
I_1(z)=\int_0^1 \frac{dx}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}\sqrt{1-zx}},
\]
show that it satisfies the second-order differential equation
\begin{equation}
\Big[\theta^2 - z\big(\theta+\tfrac12\big)^2\Big]\,I_1(z)=0,
\label{eq:ODE_N1_theta}
\end{equation}
and expand it into the standard form
\begin{equation}
z(1-z)I_1''(z)+(1-2z)I_1'(z)-\frac14 I_1(z)=0.
\label{eq:ODE_N1_std}
\end{equation}

For
\[
I_2(z)=\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 \frac{dx\,dy}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}\sqrt{y(1-y)}\sqrt{1-zxy}},
\]
show that it satisfies the third-order equation
\begin{equation}
\Big[\theta^3 - z\big(\theta+\tfrac12\big)^3\Big]\,I_2(z)=0.
\label{eq:ODE_N2_theta}
\end{equation}

Consider
\begin{align}
\frac{d}{dx}\mathbf f=
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{3-\epsilon}{x} & 0\\[4pt]
\frac{\epsilon}{x(1-x)} & \frac{\epsilon}{1-x}
\end{pmatrix}\mathbf f,\qquad 0<x<1.
\label{eq:toyA}
\end{align}
Define $f_1=x^3 g_1,\ f_2=g_2$ (i.e.\ $T(x)=\mathrm{diag}(x^3,1)$).
What is the transformed system in $\epsilon$-form?

Consider the system
\begin{align}
\frac{d}{dx}\mathbf f=
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{2-\epsilon}{x} & 0\\[6pt]
\frac{\epsilon}{1-x} & \frac{\epsilon}{1-x}
\end{pmatrix}\mathbf f,\qquad 0<x<1,
\label{eq:toyB}
\end{align}
What is the basis change to make it in canonical form?

For \eqref{eq:Vprime_model}, show that in the one-cut ansatz \eqref{eq:onecut_ansatz} it is consistent to take
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Mpoly_form}
M(z)=g z^2 + p z + q,
\end{equation}
with constants $p,q$ (depending on $g,h,a,b$).

Now set $h=0$ but keep $g>0$ in \eqref{eq:Vprime_model}, so $V'(z)=z+gz^3$ is even.
Assume the one-cut support is symmetric: $a=-A$, $b=+A$ with $A>0$.
Using \eqref{eq:onecut_ansatz} with \eqref{eq:Mpoly_form} and the asymptotic \eqref{eq:asymptotic},
derive:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:A2_result}
A^2=\frac{\sqrt{1+8g}-1}{g},
\end{equation}
and the planar density
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rho_quartic_even}
\rho_0(x)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\left(gx^2+q_0\right)\sqrt{A^2-x^2}\;\mathbf{1}_{|x|\le A},
\qquad
q_0=\frac{4}{A^2}.
\end{equation}

Show that $A_0^2=0$ and hence
\begin{equation}\label{eq:zA0}
z^{\epsilon A_0}\equiv e^{\epsilon A_0\log z}=I_2+\epsilon A_0 H_0(z)
\qquad\text{exactly (no higher powers).}
\end{equation}

